<description>
Kernel logging is inherently global: `KernelLogger` is a process-wide
singleton, so every connection receives every log entry and shares the
minimum level. Multiple `btck_LoggingConnection` handles give the false
impression of independent loggers.

Limit `btck_global_logging_connection_create` to a single connection, so
that the globalness is expressed in that one function and the rest of
the interface can operate on the connection handle.

A single connection also makes it straightforward to invoke the callback
without holding the logger's mutex. Holding it serializes all logging
threads on user code, and deadlocks if anything on the callback's thread
logs, e.g. a garbage collector running finalizers that destroy kernel
objects.

Behaviour changes for consumers:
- `btck_logging_connection_create` is renamed to
  `btck_global_logging_connection_create`, and fails if a connection
  already exists.
- `btck_logging_set_min_level` becomes
  `btck_logging_connection_set_min_level`, and each new connection
  starts at `Info`.
- The "Logger connected." `Debug` entry is removed, since it can no
  longer be delivered.
- The callback can be invoked from multiple threads at the same time,
  so it must be thread-safe. Destroying the connection still waits for
  running invocations to return.
</description>

<commits>
a93f5c019a refactor: merge KernelLogger::MaybeLog into Log

MaybeLog() is the only caller of Log().

0d1ec522a9 kernel: allow only one global logging connection

KernelLogger is a process-wide singleton, so every connection receives
every log entry and shares the minimum level. Multiple
btck_LoggingConnection handles give the false impression of independent
loggers.

Behaviour change: btck_logging_connection_create is renamed to
btck_global_logging_connection_create, and fails if a connection
already exists.

771f44ed38 kernel: set the minimum log level on the logging connection

With only one connection, the logging interface can operate on the
connection handle instead of on global state.

Behaviour changes:
- btck_logging_set_min_level becomes
  btck_logging_connection_set_min_level.
- Each new connection starts at Info, instead of at the level of the
  previous connection.
- The "Logger connected." debug entry is removed, since it can no
  longer be delivered.

921329000f kernel: invoke the logging callback without holding the lock

Holding m_mutex while running the callback serializes all logging
threads on user code, and deadlocks if anything on the callback's
thread logs, e.g. a garbage collector running finalizers that destroy
kernel objects.

Behaviour change: the callback can be invoked from multiple threads at
the same time, so it must be thread-safe. Destroying the connection
still waits for running invocations to return.

Update bitcoin-chainstate and the test logger to write each line in a
single call, so concurrent lines don't interleave.
